==Irish==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|sga|ga}} {{m|sga|cen||besides; without}}, from {{etyl|cel-pro|ga}} {{m|cel-pro|*kina||besides}}; compare Welsh {{m|cy|amgen|am-'''gen'''|otherwise}}, Breton {{m|br|ken||otherwise}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Munster|Aran}} {{IPA|lang=ga|[ɡɑn̪ˠ]}}
* {{a|Connemara|Mayo|Ulster}} {{IPA|lang=ga|[ɡan̪ˠ]}}

===Preposition===
{{ga-prep}}

# [[without]]
# [[not]] {{qualifier|in conjunction with a verbal noun}}

====Usage notes====
Triggers {{ga-mut-link|l}} of ''b, c, g, m, p'' on unmodified nouns, e.g. ''gan {{l|ga|pingin|phingin}}'' ‘without a penny’. Does not trigger lenition on modified nouns, e.g. ''gan {{l|ga|pingin}} ina phóca'' ‘without a penny in his pocket’. In the meaning ‘not’, does not trigger lenition on either a verbal noun or on the direct object of the verbal noun, e.g. ''gan {{l|ga|ceannaigh|ceannach}}'' ‘not to buy’, ''gan {{l|ga|pingin}} a shaothrú'' ‘not to earn a penny’.

Unlike most Irish prepositions, ''gan'' is followed by the nominative case of nouns, not the dative, and it does not form prepositional pronouns: ''gan {{l|ga|an}} {{l|ga|arán|t-arán}}'' ‘without the bread’, ''gan {{l|ga|mé}}'' ‘without me’.

